since the first x-men movie all the way  back in 2000 the architects of the  franchise have been dropping in small  nods and references to the comics that  inspired the film's Dark Phoenix  continues the tradition and some big and  not so big Easter eggs for the  eagle-eyed and comics versed here are  some you may have blinked and missed on  your first watch and if you haven't seen  the movie here's your warning spoilers  are ahead Dark Phoenix opens with a  tragic scene set in 1975 a pivotal year  for the x-men with the release of  giant-size x-men number one an issue  that single-handedly revitalized a  flagging Marvel franchise by bringing on  characters including Nightcrawler  Colossus and Wolverine the guy you might  have heard of before the scene turns  tragic and the greys crashed their car  an argument over what's on the radio  leads to two significant songs being  played as Jean telekinetically turns the  dial the first is the 1967 Glen Campbell  version of by the time I get to Phoenix  which should be pretty self-explanatory  the other is a little deeper in its  meaning it's werewolves of London by  Warren Zevon which was released three  years later in 1978 making it an  anachronism it seemed a reference to the  first issue of Excalibur titled war  wolf's of London  that series saw a ragtag team of former  x-men including the Rachel summers  version of Phoenix and Nightcrawler  carrying on in the United Kingdom after  they believed the rest of the team to be  dead Excalibur also stars Kitty Pryde  who seemingly makes a very quick cameo  phasing through a wall late in the movie  the movies present-day setting as it  were is 1992 the year the popular x-men  cartoon series started but while the  early 90s weren't exactly associated  with disco we still get an appearance  from the disco dancing mutant Dazzler  Dazzler belts out some dance tunes at  the outdoor party celebrating the  mission to save the NASA astronauts from  certain doom on the space shuttle  Endeavour  which notably did have its first mission  in may 1992 though she's a superhero  herself in the comics Dazzler doesn't  seem to be much more than the x-men's  personal party singer here speaking of  very specific jobs the party scene also  includes a quick appearance by recent  x-men recruit match who lights a fire  like Phoenix  got a bit of a temper it wouldn't be too  surprising if you missed the name of an  alien race that served as dark Phoenix's  ultimate antagonists since it's only  mentioned once in the whole movie  they're the d'bari a race of aliens who  in the comics look kind of like  asparagus they've appeared in a number  of comic stories over the years first in  Avengers number four and sometimes as  villains but for the purposes of Dark  Phoenix their most important appearance  was in uncanny x-men number 135 in which  a Phoenix possessed Jean Grey destroys  their home planet that association with  the Phoenix force is basically where the  movie d'bari similarities to the comic  characters end the shape-shifting powers  in the film are much more like Scrolls  jessica chastain plays the d'bari leader  who is also very different in the comics  they can't begin to comprehend what you  are even your x-men instead of desiring  the Phoenix force for the purposes of  creating new worlds the d'bari leader in  comics Luke simply wants to attack Jean  Grey and get revenge for the destruction  of his home planet he gets a pretty  sweet suit of armor and adopts the name  star hammer in one attempt but is not  successful time traveller professional  rebel part-time sleuth and all-around  cool guy Lucas Bishop actually appeared  in one x-men movie 2014's Days of Future  past he died in that movie but then his  death got erased when the timeline  changed at the end of that movie he only  manages to return in dark Phoenix in the  name of the Red Hook area electrical  utility in the scene where the x-men  arrived to confront Jean in the  neighborhood where she grew up after she  discovers her father is still alive a  truck emblazoned with the words  Bishop power is visible in the  background of several shots that may be  a nod to bishops mutant abilities while  they're not specifically electrical in  nature he can absorb and release various  forms of energy or who knows maybe the  movie version of Lucas bishops family  decided to found a public utility and  the future version of him will just run  that it's good work if you can get it  the magneto of Dark Phoenix is more or  less retired he tells Jean that he has  essentially decided that hurting people  won't do him any good anymore  so he stopped with all that giving up  his violent ways from the previous films  and opting to live on a secluded Island  that's a haven for mutants who just want  to get away from it all it's a  simple community built from old shipping  containers and other junk magneto has  likely pulled out of the ocean with his  powers this land was given to us by the  US government we have no intention of  taking it back not here for you  Magneto's new homeland isn't given a  name in the movie but it seems to be a  version of Genosha an island mutant  homeland from the comics the comics  version however is a much more imposing  place initially it was a nation where  mutants were enslaved but the x-men  toppled its regime while saving allies  in prison there later the United Nations  gave control of the country to magneto  who made it a mutants only nation it's  been the site of numerous conflicts  since then there are not many jokes in  Dark Phoenix there are almost zero  comedic moments despite earlier movies  in the franchise having a good bed of  comic relief remember when Wolverine  verified that he wasn't mystique when  Cyclops asked him about it in the first  movie it's me prove it you're a dick ok  Dark Phoenix totally plays it straight  but there's one thing he could  definitely call a sort of meta joke in  the new universe where Disney has merged  20th Century Fox as some viewers noticed  in the trailers the paramilitary group  that apprehends the x-men near the end  of the movie goes by the initials MCU  according to the patches on their  sleeves it likely stands for something  like mutant control unit in the cartoon  this organization is called the mutant  Control Agency but it's clear the  filmmakers wanted to change that one  letter to have a little fun a heavily  militarized group using futuristic  weapons and going by the initials MCU  takes these 20th Century Fox versions of  mutants away to reprogram and bend them  to their will that sure seems like some  kind of metaphor for something but also  they're not all bad when the real bad  guys attack it team up with the x-men so  that's nice  following genes sacrifice at the end of  the climactic battle we see that Charles  Xavier has moved on to live a life of  playing chess outside of cafes Hank  McCoy now staying in his beast form has  taken over as headmaster of the school  and he's given it a new name the Jean  Grey school for gifted youngsters  it's his tribute to her for choosing a  path of light and they  not so subtle jab at his old mentor the  school has born jeans name in the comics  too in the series Wolverine and the  x-men new headmasters Wolverine and  Kitty Pryde renamed the school the Jean  Grey school of higher learning after  Professor X's death one of many he has  endured in that instance it's less of a  slight toward Xavier and more of an  indication of a new generation of  students at the school which would of  course bear xavier z' name again a few  years later check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite movies are  coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

